-- Q1573: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The cost of remediating each individual control deficiency","Whether each deficiency has been formally communicated to management","Whether the deficiencies together could reasonably result in an undetected material misstatement","The total number of individual control deficiencies identified during testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor evaluates whether individually insignificant deficiencies, in combination, could result in a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement would not be prevented or detected. This requires assessing whether deficiencies affect the same accounts, assertions, or processes and whether compensating controls exist.
Wrong (A): Remediation cost is a management consideration for prioritizing fixes, not a factor in determining whether combined deficiencies constitute a material weakness.
Wrong (B): Communication status is a procedural step that occurs after the evaluation, not a criterion for assessing severity of combined deficiencies.
Wrong (D): The sheer number of deficiencies is less important than their interrelationship and combined effect on the likelihood of undetected material misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1573;

-- Q1577: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate whether compensating controls adequately mitigate risks from limited segregation","Conclude segregation is impossible and issue a scope limitation on the audit","Require the entity to hire additional personnel before completing the engagement","Disregard the segregation weakness since it is typical for small entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A105, in small entities where ideal segregation of duties is not achievable, the auditor should evaluate whether compensating controls such as owner-manager oversight are designed and operating effectively to mitigate the risks. The auditor assesses whether reviews are substantive and evidenced.
Wrong (B): Limited segregation does not constitute a scope limitation; the auditor evaluates compensating controls rather than concluding the audit cannot be completed.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to require management to hire staff; the focus is on evaluating existing controls and their effectiveness.
Wrong (D): Merely expecting limited segregation in small entities does not relieve the auditor of the obligation to assess the related risks and compensating controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1577;

-- Q1578: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Developers may fail to complete programming tasks within scheduled deadlines","Developers will encounter difficulty accessing their designated development environments","The entity''s physical hardware infrastructure could sustain operational damage","Developers could make unauthorized production changes without detection or approval"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A107, granting system administrator access to application developers violates segregation of duties between development and production environments. Developers with such access could promote untested code or modify production data without detection, undermining change management controls and application-level integrity.
Wrong (A): Task completion timeliness is a project management concern unrelated to the ITGC risk of granting excessive system access privileges.
Wrong (B): System administrator access expands rather than restricts environment access, so difficulty accessing development environments is not a resulting risk.
Wrong (C): Physical hardware damage is unrelated to logical access control weaknesses; system administrator privileges affect software and data, not physical infrastructure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1578;

-- Q1579: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit the joint venture separately as its own standalone engagement","Rely entirely on the joint venture partner''s auditor for all related matters","Evaluate the investment accounting, assess additional risks, and verify information access","Exclude the joint venture from scope because it is a separate legal entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 300.A2, during planning the auditor must evaluate the accounting for the joint venture investment, consider whether the arrangement creates additional risks of material misstatement, and determine whether sufficient access to the joint venture''s financial information exists. This includes evaluating the appropriate accounting method and related party considerations.
Wrong (A): The joint venture is part of the client''s financial statements and does not require a separate engagement; the auditor plans procedures within the existing audit.
Wrong (B): Complete reliance on another auditor without independent evaluation would not satisfy the auditor''s responsibility under AU-C 600 for group audit considerations.
Wrong (D): A joint venture''s separate legal status does not exclude it from audit scope; its impact on the client''s financial statements must be addressed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1579;

-- Q1580: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["New management has no impact because they were not involved in prior year transactions","The auditor should automatically issue a going concern opinion for management changes","The audit fee should be reduced since new management may be more cooperative","Changes in senior management may affect the control environment and tone at the top"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A79, changes in senior management can significantly affect the control environment and tone at the top. New management may have different philosophies, risk appetites, or approaches to financial reporting, requiring the auditor to reassess financial statement level risks and evaluate management''s integrity and competence.
Wrong (A): New management may alter controls, policies, and risk assessment processes, which directly affects the current year audit even if they were not involved in prior transactions.
Wrong (B): A going concern opinion is based on substantial doubt about the entity''s ability to continue, not merely on management turnover.
Wrong (C): Fee determination is unrelated to risk assessment; management cooperativeness does not reduce audit risk or justify a fee reduction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1580;

-- Q1581: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Asset nature and portability, physical controls, and employee access combined with concealment ability","Only the total value of assets reported on the balance sheet date","Only cash and cash equivalents since other assets cannot be misappropriated","Only whether the entity maintains insurance coverage for potential asset losses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 240.A30, assessing misappropriation risk requires evaluating the nature and portability of assets, the adequacy of physical safeguards and reconciliation procedures, and whether inadequate segregation of duties allows employees both access to assets and the ability to conceal theft through record manipulation.
Wrong (B): Balance sheet value alone does not capture the key risk factors; portability, physical controls, and employee access are equally important considerations for misappropriation risk.
Wrong (C): Inventory, supplies, and other portable assets can also be misappropriated; limiting the assessment to cash ignores significant categories of at-risk assets.
Wrong (D): Insurance coverage mitigates the financial impact of losses but does not address the susceptibility to misappropriation or the effectiveness of preventive controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1581;

-- Q1582: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the cash account balances denominated in foreign currencies","Only foreign currency transactions occurring in the last month of the year","Translation, remeasurement, exchange rate appropriateness, and hedge accounting","Only the accounts receivable balances with foreign currency exposure"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315.A33, significant foreign currency transactions introduce risks related to the proper application of ASC 830, including selecting appropriate exchange rates for translation and remeasurement, correctly classifying gains and losses, and evaluating hedge accounting designations. These areas involve complex judgment and accounting requirements.
Wrong (A): Foreign currency risk extends beyond cash to receivables, payables, revenue, and equity accounts; limiting the assessment to cash would miss significant exposure areas.
Wrong (B): Foreign currency transactions throughout the entire year require assessment, not only those in the final month; remeasurement applies to all reporting periods.
Wrong (D): Accounts receivable is only one of many accounts affected by foreign currency risk; payables, revenue, intercompany balances, and investments are also impacted.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1582;

-- Q1590: Audit Evidence [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Rely entirely on the predecessor''s workpapers without performing further procedures","Decline the engagement because opening balance evidence is inherently insufficient","Review the predecessor''s workpapers and perform additional procedures as necessary","Accept the predecessor''s conclusions and opening balance figures without modification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 510.08, the auditor should review the predecessor''s workpapers for evidence about opening balances and perform additional procedures as deemed necessary to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. The current auditor retains responsibility for independently evaluating the adequacy of opening balance support.
Wrong (A): Simply relying on the predecessor''s workpapers without independent evaluation would not satisfy the current auditor''s responsibility for obtaining sufficient appropriate evidence under AU-C 510.
Wrong (B): The availability of predecessor workpapers provides a starting point for evidence gathering; declining the engagement is not required when additional procedures can address any gaps.
Wrong (D): Accepting conclusions without modification removes the current auditor''s professional skepticism and independent judgment obligations required by AU-C 510.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1590;

-- Q1593: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The assessed risk of material misstatement is high and individual balances are large","The client has specifically requested that negative confirmations be used exclusively","The auditor has been unable to obtain responses to positive confirmation requests","Assessed risk is low, many small balances exist, and recipients will likely respond"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 505.16, negative confirmations are appropriate only when the assessed risk of material misstatement is low, a large number of small balances is involved, and the auditor has no reason to believe recipients will disregard the requests. All three conditions must be present for negative confirmations to provide sufficient evidence.
Wrong (A): Negative confirmations are inappropriate when risk is high or balances are large; these conditions call for positive confirmations that require explicit responses.
Wrong (B): Client preference does not determine the appropriateness of confirmation type; the auditor must independently assess whether conditions support using negative confirmations.
Wrong (C): Inability to obtain positive confirmations does not automatically justify switching to negative confirmations; alternative procedures should be considered instead.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1593;

-- Q1594: Audit Evidence [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are significantly less expensive to process and administer","They require the respondent to provide the balance, reducing careless agreement risk","They consistently achieve a higher response rate than positive confirmations","They eliminate the need to perform alternative procedures for any non-responses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 505.A7, blank confirmations require the respondent to provide the balance from their own records rather than simply agreeing to a stated amount. This reduces the risk that respondents will sign and return the confirmation without actually verifying the balance, providing more reliable evidence.
Wrong (A): Processing cost is not the primary advantage of blank confirmations; the key benefit is the quality of evidence obtained through independent balance verification.
Wrong (C): Blank confirmations typically have lower response rates because they require more effort from respondents, who must look up and fill in the balance.
Wrong (D): Non-responses to blank confirmations still require alternative procedures, just as with other types of positive confirmations under AU-C 505.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1594;

-- Q1595: Audit Evidence [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately withdraw from the engagement due to management''s restriction","Issue an adverse opinion based on the inability to confirm the balance","Inquire about management''s reasons for the refusal and evaluate their reasonableness","Confirm the balance directly with the customer without informing management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 505.08, when management refuses to allow confirmations, the auditor should first inquire about the reasons for the refusal and evaluate their reasonableness. If the reasons are valid, alternative procedures are performed under AU-C 505.10; if unreasonable, the auditor considers implications for risk assessment.
Wrong (A): Immediately withdrawing is premature without first understanding and evaluating management''s reasons; the refusal may have a legitimate business justification.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion addresses material misstatements in the financial statements, not procedural restrictions; the auditor must first evaluate the refusal and consider alternatives.
Wrong (D): Sending confirmations against management''s explicit instructions would undermine the professional relationship and may not be appropriate without first evaluating the refusal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1595;

-- Q1596: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A guarantee that no fraud has occurred during the reporting period","A listing of all adjusting journal entries proposed by the auditor","A projection of the entity''s expected future earnings and cash flows","An acknowledgment of management''s responsibility for financial statement preparation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 580.10, management must acknowledge in writing its responsibility for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements in accordance with the applicable financial reporting framework. This acknowledgment is a fundamental required written representation.
Wrong (A): Management cannot guarantee the absence of fraud; under AU-C 580.11, they can only represent that they have disclosed all known fraud or suspected fraud affecting the entity.
Wrong (B): The representation letter addresses management''s responsibilities and assertions, not the auditor''s proposed adjustments; adjusting entries are communicated through other means.
Wrong (C): Earnings projections are forward-looking estimates unrelated to the required representations about historical financial statement preparation and fair presentation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1596;

-- Q1601: Audit Evidence [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the auditor issues two separate opinions on different financial statement components","When the audit engagement spans two separate fiscal year reporting periods","When two auditors jointly sign and take responsibility for the audit report","When a specific subsequent event is disclosed and the auditor limits extended responsibility"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 560.12, dual dating is used when a material subsequent event is discovered after the original report date but before issuance. The auditor dates the report with the original date except for the note describing the subsequent event, which receives the later date. This limits the auditor''s responsibility for events between the two dates to only the specific disclosed event.
Wrong (A): Issuing separate opinions on different components relates to component auditing, not dual dating; dual dating involves a single report with two dates for different subsequent events.
Wrong (B): The audit spanning two fiscal years describes a multi-period engagement, not the dual-dating concept which addresses subsequent events between the report date and issuance.
Wrong (C): Joint signatures relate to shared audit responsibility among firms, not the dating convention used to limit subsequent event responsibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1601;

-- Q1606: Audit Evidence [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately issue an adverse opinion based on suspected fraud by management","Communicate the matter to management and evaluate the transaction''s business purpose","Withdraw from the engagement entirely without conducting further investigation","Disclose the transaction directly in the auditor''s report without consulting management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 550.A30, when a previously undisclosed related party transaction is identified, the auditor should communicate the information to the engagement team and request management to identify all related party relationships and transactions. AU-C 550.19 requires the auditor to evaluate the business purpose and substance of the transaction.
Wrong (A): Issuing an adverse opinion without first understanding the circumstances is premature; the undisclosed transaction may result from oversight rather than intentional fraud.
Wrong (C): Withdrawing without investigation does not satisfy the auditor''s obligation to gather sufficient appropriate evidence about the nature and purpose of the transaction.
Wrong (D): The auditor should first discuss the matter with management to understand the circumstances before making any reporting decisions about the transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1606;

-- Q1607: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Completeness of recorded transactions and balances","Classification and presentation in financial statements","Cutoff of transactions near the reporting period end","Valuation and allocation of the recorded amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.A10, related party transactions may not be conducted at arm''s length. When a CEO sells a building to the company at a price significantly above fair market value, the valuation and allocation assertion is most directly at risk because the recorded amount may not reflect the asset''s actual fair value. AU-C 540 requires evaluation of measurement reasonableness.
Wrong (A): Completeness relates to whether all transactions are recorded; the building purchase was recorded, so completeness is not the primary assertion at risk.
Wrong (B): Classification concerns whether the transaction is presented in the proper account category, not whether the amount is appropriate relative to fair value.
Wrong (C): Cutoff addresses whether transactions are recorded in the correct period, which is not the primary concern when the issue is an above-market purchase price.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1607;

-- Q1609: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Procedures must cover the entire period from balance sheet date through the report date","The auditor only needs to consider events occurring during the fieldwork period","Subsequent events procedures are optional unless specifically requested by the client","The auditor''s responsibility for subsequent events ends at the balance sheet date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 560.09, the auditor is responsible for performing procedures to identify events between the financial statement date and the auditor''s report date that require adjustment or disclosure. These procedures include reading board minutes, inquiring about litigation, and obtaining management representations.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s responsibility extends through the report date, not merely through the completion of fieldwork; events occurring after fieldwork but before the report date must also be evaluated.
Wrong (C): Subsequent events procedures are required by AU-C 560 as a mandatory part of every audit, not an optional procedure subject to client request.
Wrong (D): The auditor''s subsequent events responsibility extends beyond the balance sheet date through the date of the auditor''s report, as specified in AU-C 560.09.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1609;

-- Q1618: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Examining subsequent cash disbursements and tracing them to the year-end payable listing","Confirming reported account balances with the entity''s known vendor contacts","Vouching recorded payable amounts to their supporting purchase order documentation","Reviewing the aged accounts payable trial balance for unusual or stale items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 500.A32, to test completeness of accounts payable the auditor searches for unrecorded liabilities by examining payments made after year-end and tracing them to the year-end payable listing. Starting from a source outside the recorded population effectively identifies liabilities that existed at year-end but were not recorded.
Wrong (B): Confirming balances with known vendors tests existence and accuracy of recorded payables but does not effectively identify unrecorded liabilities from unknown or omitted vendors.
Wrong (C): Vouching recorded payables to purchase orders tests existence and accuracy of items already in the population, not whether additional unrecorded liabilities exist.
Wrong (D): Reviewing the aged trial balance analyzes recorded payables for reasonableness but does not identify liabilities that were never recorded in the system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1618;

-- Q1625: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Management has asserted in writing that the system is functioning properly","The information system has operated without any modifications for over ten years","The auditor has tested ITGCs and application controls and found them operating effectively","The system was purchased from a well-known and reputable technology vendor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 500.A31, the reliability of internally generated evidence depends on the effectiveness of controls over its production. When the auditor tests IT general controls (access, change management, operations) and application controls (input, processing, output) and finds them operating effectively, confidence in the evidence produced by the system is enhanced.
Wrong (A): Management assertions about system functioning are representations, not evidence of control effectiveness; the auditor must independently test controls to assess reliability.
Wrong (B): A system operating unchanged for a long period does not ensure reliability; outdated systems may have unpatched vulnerabilities or controls that no longer address current risks.
Wrong (D): A vendor''s reputation does not provide assurance about how the system is configured, maintained, or controlled in the specific client environment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1625;

-- Q1626: Audit Evidence [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The specialist''s competence, objectivity, and appropriateness of their work as evidence","Only whether the specialist''s final report is properly formatted and organized","Whether the specialist has received formal approval or registration from the PCAOB","Only the mathematical accuracy of the calculations the specialist has performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 500.08, the auditor evaluates the competence, capabilities, and objectivity of management''s specialist, the appropriateness of the specialist''s work as audit evidence for the relevant assertion, and whether the assumptions, methods, and source data used are reasonable and relevant to the engagement.
Wrong (B): Report formatting is a superficial consideration; the auditor must evaluate the substance of the specialist''s work, including methods, assumptions, and source data used.
Wrong (C): The PCAOB does not approve or register management''s specialists such as actuaries or appraisers; the auditor independently evaluates their competence and objectivity.
Wrong (D): Mathematical accuracy is only one aspect of evaluation; the auditor must also assess the reasonableness of assumptions, methods, and whether the specialist is competent and objective.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1626;

-- Q1627: Audit Evidence [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the representation at face value because it bears management''s signature","Investigate the inconsistency and reconsider the reliability of all representations","Immediately resign from the engagement without performing further procedures","Issue a disclaimer of opinion without conducting any additional investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.17, when a written representation is inconsistent with other audit evidence, the auditor must investigate the contradiction and consider whether it casts doubt on the reliability of other management representations. The auditor should perform additional procedures to resolve the matter and may need to reconsider the risk assessment.
Wrong (A): A signature does not make a representation reliable when contradicted by other evidence; the auditor must exercise professional skepticism and investigate inconsistencies.
Wrong (C): Immediate resignation is premature without first investigating the inconsistency; the contradiction may have a reasonable explanation that does not indicate management dishonesty.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer of opinion is a last resort when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence; investigation of the contradiction must occur before any reporting decision.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1627;

-- Q1628: Audit Evidence [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A new product line is launched by the entity after the balance sheet date","A new bond issuance is completed by the entity after the balance sheet date","An earthquake destroys a company warehouse after the balance sheet date","A customer owing money at year-end files bankruptcy, confirming receivable impairment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 560.09, a customer bankruptcy after year-end that confirms impairment existing at the balance sheet date is a Type I (recognized) subsequent event. The condition existed at year-end and the post-year-end event provides additional evidence, requiring adjustment of the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Launching a new product line is a Type II (nonrecognized) subsequent event representing a new condition arising after year-end that may require disclosure but not adjustment.
Wrong (B): A new bond issuance is a Type II event representing a new financing arrangement that did not exist at the balance sheet date; it requires disclosure rather than adjustment.
Wrong (C): An earthquake destroying a warehouse after year-end is a Type II event because the loss-causing condition did not exist at the balance sheet date; it may require disclosure only.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1628;

-- Q1629: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["That related party transactions will likely increase the client''s overall tax liability","That the client has too many related parties to adequately disclose in notes","That related party transactions are inherently fraudulent and must be eliminated","That transactions may lack arm''s length terms and genuine economic substance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.A10, the primary risk with related party transactions is that they may not be conducted at arm''s length and the terms may differ from those agreed between unrelated parties. Additionally, some transactions may lack genuine economic substance and could be used to manipulate financial results or obscure the true nature of arrangements.
Wrong (A): Tax implications are a potential consequence but not the primary audit risk; the auditor''s concern is whether transactions are properly valued, accounted for, and disclosed.
Wrong (B): The number of related parties is not the concern; rather, the auditor focuses on whether the transactions with those parties are conducted on fair terms and properly disclosed.
Wrong (C): Related party transactions are not inherently fraudulent; many are legitimate business transactions, but they require additional scrutiny because of the non-arm''s-length risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1629;

-- Q1630: Audit Evidence [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confirming the specific warranty terms and conditions directly with customers","Testing inventory counts of parts designated for warranty replacement purposes","Reviewing product satisfaction surveys prepared by the marketing department","Comparing the estimate to actual prior-period claims and evaluating key assumptions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 540.13, to evaluate a warranty estimate the auditor should compare management''s current estimate against actual historical warranty experience, evaluate the reasonableness of assumptions about future claim rates and costs, and consider changes in product quality or warranty terms that might affect the estimate.
Wrong (A): Confirming warranty terms with customers verifies contractual obligations but does not address whether the dollar amount of the reserve is reasonable based on expected claims.
Wrong (B): Testing inventory counts of replacement parts addresses inventory existence but does not evaluate the reasonableness of the estimated future cost of warranty claims.
Wrong (C): Marketing satisfaction surveys are not a reliable basis for evaluating warranty cost estimates; historical claims data and actuarial assumptions are more relevant evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1630;

-- Q1636: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Statistical sampling always produces more accurate results than nonstatistical sampling","Nonstatistical sampling is prohibited under professional auditing standards","Statistical sampling does not require any exercise of professional judgment","Statistical sampling uses random selection and mathematically measures sampling risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 530.05, statistical sampling uses random selection methods and applies probability theory to evaluate results, providing a mathematical measurement of sampling risk. Nonstatistical sampling relies on the auditor''s professional judgment for both sample selection and evaluation of results.
Wrong (A): Neither method is inherently more accurate; AU-C 530.A1 confirms both can provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence when properly designed and executed.
Wrong (B): Nonstatistical sampling is expressly permitted under AU-C 530; auditing standards recognize both statistical and nonstatistical approaches as acceptable.
Wrong (C): Statistical sampling still requires professional judgment in areas such as defining the population, setting tolerable misstatement, and evaluating results.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1636;

-- Q1639: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Every dollar has an equal selection chance, so larger balances are more likely sampled","It is the only sampling method permitted for conducting tests of controls","It automatically detects all instances of fraud present within the population","It completely eliminates the need for any population stratification techniques"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530.A19, MUS treats each individual dollar as a sampling unit, giving every dollar an equal probability of selection. As explained in AU-C 530.A20, items with larger recorded amounts have a proportionally higher chance of inclusion, focusing audit effort on the largest items with the greatest misstatement potential.
Wrong (B): MUS is primarily used for substantive testing of account balances, not tests of controls; attribute sampling is typically used for control testing.
Wrong (C): No sampling method automatically detects all fraud; MUS is designed to test for overstatement of recorded balances, not to serve as a comprehensive fraud detection tool.
Wrong (D): While MUS provides a form of built-in stratification by weighting selection toward larger items, this is an inherent feature, not a general elimination of stratification needs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1639;

-- Q1640: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Items with small or zero recorded amounts have little or no chance of being selected","It requires the auditor to rely exclusively on judgment-based sample selection","It is not recognized as a statistically valid sampling technique by standards","It can only be applied to populations containing fewer than one hundred items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530.A19, MUS selects individual dollars rather than individual items, so accounts with small or zero recorded balances have little or no chance of selection. AU-C 530.A20 explains this makes MUS less effective at detecting understatements, since an unrecorded liability would have zero probability of selection.
Wrong (B): MUS uses systematic or random dollar-unit selection, not judgment-based selection; the method is statistically grounded and objective.
Wrong (C): MUS is a recognized statistical sampling technique under AU-C 530; it applies probability theory to evaluate results and measure sampling risk.
Wrong (D): MUS is well-suited for large populations with many items; there is no upper or lower limit on population size for its application.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1640;

-- Q1642: Audit Sampling [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control is operating effectively because 3% is below the 5% tolerable rate","The control has completely failed and must be abandoned by the entity","No meaningful conclusion can be drawn from attribute sampling test results","The auditor must consider the achieved upper deviation rate before concluding"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 530.13, when evaluating sample results the auditor must consider sampling risk. Even though the sample deviation rate (3%) is below the tolerable rate (5%), AU-C 530.A29 requires calculating the achieved upper deviation rate, which accounts for the possibility that the true population rate exceeds the sample rate. If it exceeds 5%, the planned reliance cannot be supported.
Wrong (A): Comparing only the sample deviation rate to the tolerable rate ignores sampling risk; the achieved upper deviation rate may exceed the tolerable rate despite a lower sample rate.
Wrong (B): Three deviations in 100 items does not mean the control has completely failed; it indicates a need for further statistical evaluation before drawing conclusions.
Wrong (C): Attribute sampling is specifically designed to support conclusions about control effectiveness; stating no conclusion can be drawn contradicts the purpose of the technique.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1642;

-- Q1646: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls are judged ineffective when actually effective, causing unnecessary substantive testing","Controls are judged effective when they are actually operating ineffectively","The auditor fails to detect a material misstatement in the financial statements","The auditor issues an incorrect opinion on the entity''s financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530.A1, the risk of assessing control risk too high (underreliance) is the risk that sample results fail to support planned reliance on controls when the true operating deviation rate is actually below the tolerable rate. This leads to unnecessary expansion of substantive procedures, affecting audit efficiency rather than effectiveness.
Wrong (B): Concluding controls are effective when they are not describes the risk of assessing control risk too low (overreliance), which is a different sampling risk affecting audit effectiveness.
Wrong (C): Failing to detect a material misstatement relates to detection risk and audit effectiveness, not the efficiency-related risk of assessing control risk too high.
Wrong (D): Issuing an incorrect opinion is a consequence of overall audit failure, not the specific definition of the risk of assessing control risk too high.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1646;

-- Q1649: Audit Sampling [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because the sample deviation rate of 0% is well below the tolerable rate","No, because a sample of 60 items is too small to draw any meaningful conclusion","No, because finding zero deviations is considered statistically impossible in practice","Yes, but only if the achieved upper deviation rate is at or below the tolerable rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 530.A16, even when zero deviations are found in a sample of 60, the auditor cannot simply conclude the deviation rate is zero. The auditor must consider the achieved upper deviation rate, which accounts for sampling risk. For a sample of 60 with zero deviations at a 5% risk of overreliance, the achieved upper deviation rate is approximately 5%.
Wrong (A): Comparing only the sample deviation rate to the tolerable rate ignores the achieved upper deviation rate and the inherent uncertainty of sampling that must be considered.
Wrong (B): A sample of 60 items can support valid statistical conclusions; sample sufficiency depends on the tolerable rate and confidence level, not an arbitrary minimum size.
Wrong (C): Zero deviations is a legitimate and common statistical outcome, particularly when controls are operating effectively; it is not impossible or inherently suspect.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1649;

-- Q1650: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The total amount of misstatement actually present in the financial statements overall","The misstatement threshold that will always result in a qualified audit opinion","The maximum population misstatement the auditor will accept as fairly stated","The expected misstatement level based on the results of prior year audit testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530.05 and AU-C 530.A3, tolerable misstatement is a monetary amount set by the auditor representing the maximum misstatement in a population that the auditor is willing to accept and still conclude the account is not materially misstated. It represents the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure.
Wrong (A): Tolerable misstatement is a threshold set by the auditor for a specific account population, not the total actual misstatement in the financial statements as a whole.
Wrong (B): Tolerable misstatement is a sampling concept that guides sample size and evaluation, not a fixed threshold that automatically triggers a qualified opinion.
Wrong (D): Expected misstatement is a separate concept used in sample size planning; tolerable misstatement is the maximum acceptable amount, not a prediction of what will be found.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1650;
